export function Security(){ return <><PageHeader eyebrow="SECURITY" title="Security architecture for tenant isolation." intro="Agata Proxima is built around a simple security premise: tenant isolation should be enforced, tested and inspectable."/><Section title="Security model"><div className="security-list">{[["Identity","Authentication establishes the principal; tenant scope is handled separately."],["Tenant context","Tenant identity travels explicitly through the request path."],["Enforcement","The boundary decides whether the request can reach protected tenant data."],["Database controls","PostgreSQL provides another enforcement layer."],["Verification","Expected allow/block behavior is tested rather than assumed."],["Evidence","Results become inspectable operational and security evidence."]].map(([a,b])=><article key={a}><strong>{a}</strong><p>{b}</p></article>)}</div></Section><Section eyebrow="THREAT MODEL" title="Design for the mistakes that cause isolation failures."><div className="three-columns"><article><h3>Wrong tenant context</h3><p>A request identifies the wrong tenant and attempts to cross the boundary.</p></article><article><h3>Expired context</h3><p>A context that should no longer authorize access is presented to the system.</p></article><article><h3>Missing enforcement</h3><p>A service path bypasses an application convention and reaches protected data without the expected tenant check.</p></article></div></Section><Section title="Responsible disclosure"><div className="prose-wide"><p>If you believe you have found a security issue, use the security contact path rather than publicly disclosing sensitive details.</p><Link className="text-link" to="/contact">Contact the security team →</Link></div></Section></>}

const developerPages: Record<string,{title:string;intro:string;sections:[string,string,string][]}> = {
  quickstart:{title:"Quickstart",intro:"Understand the integration path before connecting Proxima to your application.",sections:[["1. Identify the tenant","Decide what the tenant boundary means in your application.","Document the organization or customer identifier that should govern protected access."],["2. Establish identity","Authenticate the calling principal.","Authentication answers who is making the request. Keep that separate from the tenant scope the request is allowed to use."],["3. Carry tenant context","Make the intended tenant explicit.","The request path should contain enough context for the enforcement layer to evaluate the intended tenant."],["4. Verify","Exercise allow and block cases.","Verify same-tenant access and deliberately test cross-tenant and invalid-context paths."],["5. Inspect evidence","Review the result.","Use the resulting verification and audit information to understand what the system actually observed."]]},
  authentication:{title:"Authentication",intro:"Authentication establishes the principal. Proxima uses that identity as one input to tenant-aware enforcement.",sections:[["Separate concerns","Identity is not tenant scope.","Do not treat a successful login as proof that the request may access every tenant the user could potentially name."],["Session boundary","Keep authenticated access protected.","The customer control plane remains behind the existing authentication boundary. Public documentation and support never bypass it."],["Tenant decision","Evaluate the requested scope.","The authenticated principal and tenant context must produce an authorization decision that remains within the intended boundary."]]},
  "tenant-context":{title:"Tenant Context",intro:"Tenant context makes the tenant decision explicit instead of leaving it implicit in application conventions.",sections:[["Purpose","Carry tenant scope through the request path.","A request should make clear which tenant it intends to operate on so downstream enforcement can compare that scope with protected resources."],["Integrity","Do not trust an arbitrary tenant identifier.","Tenant context needs an integrity model so a caller cannot simply change a value and become another tenant."],["Failure","Invalid context must fail closed.","Expired, malformed or otherwise invalid context should result in a block rather than an unscoped fallback."]]},
  verification:{title:"Verification",intro:"Verification tests the property that tenant A cannot reach tenant B data.",sections:[["Expected cases","Define the contract.","Same-tenant requests should be allowed. Cross-tenant requests and expired context should be blocked."],["Negative testing","Test the dangerous paths.","Verification should deliberately attempt the requests that would expose an isolation weakness if the boundary were missing."],["Evidence","Keep the result.","A verification run should produce enough information to understand what was tested and whether the expected result occurred."]]},
  "api-reference":{title:"API Reference",intro:"A structured reference for the concepts and operations exposed by Proxima. Endpoint availability must match the deployed control-plane implementation.",sections:[["Authentication","Understand how callers establish identity.","Use the authentication model documented for the environment you are integrating with. Do not place secrets in browser code."],["Tenant-aware operations","Keep tenant scope explicit.","Operations that access protected tenant resources should identify the intended scope and be evaluated by the enforcement boundary."],["Errors","Treat blocks as security decisions.","A blocked request is not merely a generic failure. The client should surface enough information to diagnose the issue without leaking protected data."]]},
  webhooks:{title:"Webhooks",intro:"Use event delivery to connect Proxima decisions and lifecycle changes to your systems when webhook support is enabled.",sections:[["Event design","Know what an event represents.","Events should be treated as notifications about a state change or recorded result, not as a substitute for authoritative state."],["Verification","Validate incoming events.","Consumers should authenticate and validate event integrity before acting on a webhook."],["Retries","Design for repeated delivery.","Webhook consumers should be idempotent because network failures can cause a valid event to be delivered more than once."]]},
  sdks:{title:"SDKs",intro:"Use SDKs to keep integration conventions close to the language and runtime your application already uses.",sections:[["Installation","Choose the supported SDK for your environment.","Install the package using the package manager appropriate to your application and pin versions in production."],["Configuration","Keep credentials server-side.","API credentials and sensitive tenant-enforcement configuration should not be embedded in public browser bundles."],["Verification","Test your integration.","Use the verification model to prove that SDK calls preserve the intended tenant boundary."]]},
  cli:{title:"CLI",intro:"The Proxima CLI is intended to make developer and operational workflows reproducible from the terminal.",sections:[["Authentication","Use secure local credentials.","Keep local credentials outside source control and use environment-specific configuration."],["Commands","Prefer inspectable operations.","Commands should make it possible to inspect configuration, trigger supported workflows and retrieve results without hiding important state."],["Automation","Use CI carefully.","CI credentials should be scoped to the job and rotated according to your organization's security practices."]]},
  terraform:{title:"Terraform",intro:"Infrastructure-as-code should make Proxima configuration repeatable without turning secrets into configuration files.",sections:[["Provider","Configure the integration.","Use the provider and version supported by the actual Proxima distribution you deploy."],["Resources","Model desired state.","Keep tenant, environment and infrastructure configuration declarative where the implementation supports it."],["Secrets","Never commit credentials.","Use your CI or secret-management system for sensitive values rather than hard-coding them in Terraform files."]]},
};
